    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Still Face Behavioral Coding: Variable Dictionary\"\ntoc-depth: 2\nbibliography: references.bib\neditor:\n  markdown:\n    wrap: 72\n---\n\n# Project Overview\n\nThis portfolio project overviews the transformation of **continuous\nbehavioral coding from the 6-month Still Face Paradigm** into analysis\nready variables describing infant behavior, caregiver behavior, and\ndyadic interaction across Baseline, Still Face, and Reunion episodes.\n\nThe **Variable Dictionary is the main landing page**. It explains the\ntask, shows the structure of the raw event-log data, and illustrates how\nthe coding system maps onto the scored variables used for analysis. The\ncompanion [Raw Import & Scoring Pipeline](6M_Still_Face_Raw_Import.qmd)\npage documents the technical workflow used to:\n\n- validate each subject's file,\n- identify episode boundaries,\n- score durations/counts/joint-attention measures,\n- reshape the data, and\n- run quality-control checks before the resulting dataset is handed off\n  for final scoring.\n\n::: {.callout-note appearance=\"simple\" icon=\"false\"}\n### Portfolio Navigation\n\n**1. Variable Dictionary & Project Overview** → **2. Raw Import &\nScoring Pipeline**\n\n[Variable Dictionary](variable-dictionary.qmd) · [Raw Import & Scoring\nPipeline](6M_Still_Face_Raw_Import.qmd)\n:::\n\nIf you are reviewing the technical implementation, continue to the [Raw\nImport & Scoring\nPipeline](6M_Still_Face_Raw_Import.qmd#sec-technical-workflow). The\nimport page also contains a standalone summary so it can be read\ndirectly without first reviewing this dictionary.\n\n# Still Face Paradigm\n\nThe Still Face Paradigm [@tronick] is a brief, structured interaction\ntask used to study early social-emotional regulation and the\ncaregiver-infant relationship. It has three phases, or episodes, which\nare described below:\n\n- **Baseline (bl)** - the mother engages in normal face-to-face play\n  with her infant, establishing each dyad's typical interactive style\n- *a 15 second transition, where the mother looks away before returning\n  her gaze towards her infant*\n- **Still Face (sf)** - holding a neutral, unmoving expression for a set\n  interval, while remaining present but unresponsive\n- **Reunion (re)** - the mother resumes normal interaction, and the\n  dyad's recovery is observed\n\nBecause infants expect consistent, responsive interaction, the temporary\nwithdrawal of maternal responsiveness acts as a mild stressor that\nreveals how the infant regulates distress and how the mother-infant dyad\nrepairs afterward.\n\nTrained coders capture these interactions by continuously marking the\nonset and offset of discrete behavioral states (e.g., gaze direction,\nfacial affect, vocalization, and motor behavior) throughout each\nepisode. The resulting raw event logs contain timestamped start/stop\nevents for each behavior and subject. The import pipeline validates and\ntransforms those event-level records into the scored variables\nsummarized in the dictionary below.\n\n::: {.callout-tip appearance=\"simple\" icon=\"false\"}\n**Scientific relevance of Individual and dyadic differences**: the\n*magnitude* of the still-face effect and the *speed/completeness* of\nreunion recovery are used as indicators of infant self-regulation,\nmother-infant synchrony, and attachment-relevant processes. Blunted\nstill-face reactivity, minimal recovery, or atypical patterns (e.g.,\ndisorganized behavior, dysregulated affect) have been linked in the\nliterature to risk factors such as maternal depression, prenatal\nsubstance exposure, and early adversity.\n:::\n\n## Raw Data Example\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\n# Load packages used to import the raw Excel example and format the portfolio tables\nlibrary(readxl)\nlibrary(flextable)\nlibrary(officer)\nlibrary(dplyr)\n```\n:::\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"tabwid\"><style>.cl-de0843a6{}.cl-de01fdca{font-family:'Arial